is there any chance of saving the iran  nuclear deal new negotiations between  the delegation from tehran and world  powers  began in vienna today if you remember  that deal that was signed back in 2015  which is known as the jcpoa it was aimed  to curb iran's nuclear program and to  prevent it from developing nuclear  weapons in return for an easing of  economic sanctions u.s president donald  trump quit the deal three years ago iran  then started enriching uranium beyond  the levels allowed in the original deal  u.s president biden says he wants to  return to that deal  today's talks bring together iran with  germany france britain russia and china  the european countries say iran's latest  demands are unacceptable they're calling  on tehran to bring more realistic  proposals to the table iran says that  western nations have failed to come up  with any constructive proposals  for more we want to bring in trader  parsi he's the executive vice president  of the quincy institute for responsible  statecraft he joins me from washington  d.c it's good to see you again  trinidad the chairman  of the talks today  i'm said that he detected what he called  a renewed sense of purpose at the talks  he's being very generous with his  optimism here isn't he  he has in the past and i think at the  end of the day you d you do need  optimism in order to be able to see this  through uh not in the sense that it  would lead to any naivete but you need  to have people at the table that  understand the absolute necessity  of succeeding diplomatically which means  that you keep on trying you keep on  trying because the alternative is so bad  but yeah i do believe that his optimism  or or his uh renewed sense of purpose is  justified because at the end of the day  i do think that there has been some  shifts uh uh in the iranian position  that opens up some more space and  enables these talks to continue but  there also needs to be shifts on the  western side so ultimately we will not  see an agreement on less both sides so  greater flexibility and creativity  all right well you say shifts i want to  be clear here about expectations iran is  not willing to return to the old deal i  mean they've already enriched uranium  they have no plans to go back do they  actually that's not correct the iranians  are in the deal it's the united states  that left the deal the iranians are  inside the deal they're not living up to  all of the obligations of the deal  and they're using a paragraph within the  deal that says that if one side is not  performing other side can also then  reduce their obligations that paragraph  is not interpreted the same way by the  europeans but anyway the point being  they're inside the deal the u.s are the  ones that are outside but the iranians  need to go back to full compliance  i'm not seeing any indications that  they're not willing to do so i think  from there and the problem is that the  sanctions relief from the u.s side is  too unstable and too uncertain even if  the deal were to be lifted today  according to a former  french ambassador european companies  would not go back into the iranian  market because what they're looking to  see is who will win the presidency in  2025. they're not in the mood of going  back into iran and they get kicked out  again in 2025. that creates a major  problem because that means that the  economic benefit that the iranians are  looking for may not be coming until  three years from now  we know that the united states plans to  send a delegation this coming weekend is  that is that going to achieve anything i  mean the iranians they they don't want  to talk directly with the americans do  they  no and that's a very very big problem i  think a lot of these different things at  least could be proceeding faster if the  iranians agreed to talk directly to the  united states and i i hope the iranians  themselves recognize that that was a  mistake not to do it but it is important  for the united states to be there  because if there are developments uh  between the europeans and the iranians  that uh  the united states needs to react to it i  mean the europeans are essentially  acting as a an intermediary between the  u.s and iran so it's very important for  the u.s team to be there as well  i mean we know that the talks in vienna  are not the only game going on  the u.s and the israeli defense chiefs  they have also been talking what  messages are they sending  uh the wrong message in my view uh  they're sending the message or they're  trying to send the message that the  united states believes that there is a  military option and if the iranians are  not doing what the u.s asked them to do  at the negotiating table the united  states will shift towards a military  approach i think that is an absolute  mistake we've gone through this many  times before those type of threats are  as ineffective on the iranians as the  iranian escalation is ineffective on the  united states we should not be in that  territory at all we should be focusing  entirely on making sure that diplomacy  works rather than thinking that coercion  is going to be what is going to break  the deadlock here well we will be  watching these talks very closely  trinidad parsley the institute for  responsible statecraft in washington  trade as always we appreciate your time  and your insights thank you
 

